Haakon Nilsen, cunningly disguised as haakonn@operamail.com wrote:

> I've been playing wizard for some time now.  Spells are a lot of fun,
> but my games never last.  I rarely get below AC 5, so even at
> XP level 10 i'm losing hit points fast.

Perhaps your level is too high for your armour class... Try not to gain
levels so fast, but let your pet do the killing.

> I can't use most of the armor I'm used to from barbarians et al, since
> they impact my spellcasting abilities.

Don't rely on amour class. Instead, rely on avoiding combat. Let your pet
do the killing, hide behind it. *Only* kill from a distance: throw daggers
or darts, use force bolt. Try to limit forcebolting to emergencies, when
something slips by your pet and threatens you. If a monster manages to
stand next to you, use hand-to-hand combat, to preserve your power points.

> A couple of times
> I've been given wishes by water demons, and I've gotten +3 and +4
> silver dragon scale mail which brought me well below AC 0.  That was
> great, but it's a little hard to rely on water demons and quaffing
> fountains all the time :)

Avoid fountains: they harm you more than the benefit you, especially with
a low-level character.

> I can always try to get a magic marker +
> blank scrolls + knowledge of enchant armor, but I usually have to play
> a while to get all that, and it's in that early period of a game that
> I'm most vulnerable.

You shouldn't rely on luck as a strategy.

> What do you do to survive as a wizard?  Do you have to largely depend on
> luck?

I don't depend on luck. Every class has its own "best" strategies (or
several ones). You're actually playing a barbarian with spellcasting
capabilities, it seems. A barbarian *can* take the risk of quaffing from
fountains: they carry few magic items, which are often worthless when they
get cursed, and can often deal with the monsters that come from meddling
with them.

Wizards should be played with caution, as described above. Don't quaff
from fountains, don't try out unknown potions or scrolls, try to avoid
physical combat (kill monsters from afar or let your pet take care of
them), and don't be afraid to run away often: better chicken than die
(although chickens tend to die a lot in real life lately).

